WAR is a cross between Capture-the-Flag and touch typing drill.

It is a quick, (compared to Empire), territorial conquest game in which two
players participate.  Each player tries to occupy the oponent's capital while
protecting his or her own.


WAR COMMANDS
--- --------
In the following list of commands "#" is used to represent an arbitrary
number and "XY" is used to represent a location coordinate.  "XY" is of
the form  letter number; e.g. "c5".

All WAR commands are followed by a Carriage Return <CR>.

?               Redraw the screen.

a XY D          Attack from location XY in the direction D.
	"D" is one of the letters 'l', 'r', 'u', or 'd', standing
	for "left", "right", "up" or "down".
	The battle rages until one side is vanquished (which can result
	from one side retreating) at which point half of the victor's
	troops move into the contested spot.

m # XY D        Move # many troops from location XY in the direction D.
	"#" is a one or two digit decimal number; if it is larger than the
	number of troops in location XY they will all be moved.

m # XY DE...    Move # many troops from location XY in the direction D,
	leave one military there and move the rest in the direction E.
	Up to five directions can be specified at once.
	E.g. "m 9 b2 rrrrr" will move 9 troops out of b2, leave one in c2,
	one in d2, one in e2, one in f2 and five in g2.

p XY            Drop a paratrooper into location XY.  You may drop a
	paratrooper anywhere on the board.  He (she) will appear for two
	iterations as a "P" on the left side of the cell and then land.
	When the paratrooper lands she (he) will fight with any enemy
	forces already there or simply occupy the cell.

s XY            Fire an artillery shell at location XY.  You may fire a
	shell anywhere on the board except the enemy's capital.  The shell
	will appear for two iterations as an "S" on the left side of the cell
	and then hit.  A shell does 50% damage in the cell in which it hits.

t # XY AB       Transfer # many troops from location XY to location AB.
	The two locations, (XY and AB), MUST be connected by a chain of
	occupied spots for the transfer to take place.

Note that spaces are optional in all commands;
	e.g. "m6a1urr" and "m 6 a1 urr" are equivalent.

When the program is first run it will ask how many paratroops and how many
shells you wish to employ.  It costs $4,000 to outfit one paratrooper and
$8,000 for each shell.  Your budget for the war is $99,000; any money not
spent on paratroops and shells is spent outfitting foot soldiers at $1,000
each.


INITIALIZATION
--------------

Whichever program starts first will be asked to specify the board size and
the "delay".  The delay that the program requests is the time (in multiples
of two seconds) between commands.  A value of 2 or 3 seems reasonable.



AWAR
----

Awar is an automated WAR opponent that plays the usual good-tactical and
lousy-strategic game that many automata do.

The syntax for running awar is:   awar [XY]
If XY is specified it is the location of the capital.  When running awar
against awar one of the two must have the capital positioned in this way,
(the other will select a capital location based on the opponent's
capital).
